  "rounds": {
    "1": {
      "note": "These are the \"who\" words — the people in every Cantonese sentence, and your very first building blocks. Good news for English speakers: they barely change. 我 (ngo5) is both \"I\" and \"me\"; 你 (nei5) is \"you\" whoever is doing what. To talk about more than one person, add 哋 (dei6); to show something belongs to someone, add 嘅 (ge3). Learn these few and you can already point to everyone around you.",
      "words": [
        {
          "id": "pronouns-001",
          "c": "我",
          "j": "ngo5",
          "e": "I / me"
        },
        {
          "id": "pronouns-002",
          "c": "你",
          "j": "nei5",
          "e": "you"
        },
        {
          "id": "pronouns-003",
          "c": "佢",
          "j": "keoi5",
          "e": "he / she / it"
        },
        {
          "id": "pronouns-004",
          "c": "我哋",
          "j": "ngo5 dei6",
          "e": "we / us"
        },
        {
          "id": "pronouns-005",
          "c": "你哋",
          "j": "nei5 dei6",
          "e": "you (more than one)"
        },
        {
          "id": "pronouns-006",
          "c": "佢哋",
          "j": "keoi5 dei6",
          "e": "they / them"
        },
        {
